Are you going to use the same allophone for all occurrences of a letter? Th=
at=20
would seem pointless. Some phonemes have allophones, and I at least don't=
=20
pronounce them the same way all the time:
"n" can be [n] or [=C5=8B]. I usually pronounce it [=C5=8B] before "x", "g"=
, and "k",=20
and [n] elsewhere, but for some words such as "tcunCIN" I make an exception=
.
"r" can be any of several rhotics. I usually tap or roll it except when it'=
s=20
consonantal, in which case it's like English "burble", or preceded by "x", =
in=20
which case both sounds come out as simultaneous uvular and alveolar trills=
=20
distinguished only by voice. I've met people at jbovlaste who don't roll=20
their r's.
"'" can be [h] or [=CE=B8]. I say [h] in all words except sometimes "fu'ark=
a"=20
(and "fu'orka", which is a variant thereof). I've heard that some people us=
e =20
[=CE=B8] generally, but I don't remember if I've met them.

